The sensor was never told to speak C-PHY, and its own silicon says what was being asked of it was impossible

The whole C-PHY branch (patches 0088-0091) put the RECEIVER in three phases. The
TRANSMITTER was told nothing: `imx682_global` still writes

    { 0x0111, 0x02 },

inherited from the MediaTek port. And that register is not a yes/no flag. The
CCS standard defines it with values, and the definition is in the very kernel
being compiled, drivers/media/i2c/ccs/ccs-regs.h:

    #define CCS_R_CSI_SIGNALING_MODE           CCI_REG8(0x0111)
    #define CCS_CSI_SIGNALING_MODE_CSI_2_DPHY    2U
    #define CCS_CSI_SIGNALING_MODE_CSI_2_CPHY    3U

So 0x02 is D-PHY. C-PHY is 0x03.

I had read that 0x02 from the chip and taken it as "the sensor confirms it is
C-PHY". It was the other way round, and besides it was not an answer from the
sensor: it was my own write read back.

WHAT THE SENSOR DOES ANSWER, AND IT CLOSES THE CASE

The capability registers of the 0x1600 block are read-only, fixed by the
silicon. Asked of the IMX682 with the I2C validated in the same session -the
0x0016 identifier came out 0x0682, the correct one-:

    0x1602 = 0x0C    BIT(2)|BIT(3)  ->  it knows D-PHY AND C-PHY
    0x1601 = 0x0B    BIT(0)|BIT(1)|BIT(3)
                     ->  in D-PHY it supports 1, 2 and 4 lanes. NOT THREE.
    0x0111 = 0x02    ->  but it was set to D-PHY
    0x0114 = 0x02    ->  with THREE lanes

The combination that was being asked of it -three-lane D-PHY- is the only one
that its own silicon declares it does NOT know how to do.

And that ties up all the loose ends at once:

  - The vendor file asks for three lanes (laneAssign = 0x0210, and the five mode
    tables write 0x0114 = 0x02). If three-lane D-PHY does not exist, three can
    only be C-PHY, where they are three TRIOS. The conclusion "the rear camera
    is C-PHY" was right; the reasoning I sustained it with was not.

  - It explains why three-lane D-PHY never worked either, which was the end
    left dangling: it is not that it was badly tuned, it is that that
    configuration does not exist in this chip.

  - It explains the signature measured on round 26. The transmitter was in
    D-PHY (wrong, with an impossible lane count) and the receiver was listening
    in C-PHY. The PHY sees electrical activity and the frame boundary
    transitions -four per frame-, does not latch a single header sync, and that
    is why nothing reaches the CSID, not even an error.

THE PARAMETER IS PART OF THE MEASUREMENT

senal_phy allows forcing 2 or 3 at runtime. With it both configurations can be
measured IN THE SAME BOOT, with the same pipeline and the same PHY, and then the
difference is attributable to this register and to nothing else. A reboot in
between always leaves the doubt that something else may have changed.

    senal_phy = 0   the table's value (now 0x03, C-PHY)
                2   D-PHY  -> nothing must arrive, still
                3   C-PHY
